     "Dear modellers!\r<br>\r<br>I have Constant Effort mist netting data and I want to fit an\r<br>integrated model with pop. growth, recruitment and survival. I am just\r<br>looking into time-symmetric Jolly-Seber models. In Marc &amp; Michaels\r<br>awesome book BPA it is chapter 10.3.1 and 10.3.3 (basically, these\r<br>models are the same or similar to Time-symmetric open models used in\r<br>MARK - see MARK book chapter 13). These models use the capture history\r<br>to estimate both recruitment and survival (and pop. growth) at the\r<br>same time.\r<br>\r<br>The problem arises when there are missing data in the capture history\r<br>(some sites were not active in some years, ie there are NAs in capture\r<br>history instead of zeroes). I did a simple experiment with the BPA\r<br>10.3.1 and 10.3.3 examples - in 2/3 of the sites I rewrote the first 5\r<br>occasions of the capture history to NA. The recruitment parameters and\r<br>the abundance were strongly biased in first 5 occasions (see the\r<br>results below).\r<br>\r<br>This is almost certainly a result of the &quot;strange&quot; parametrization of\r<br>recruitment in the time-symmetric models. If we had only to compute\r<br>recruitment, we would just simply reverse the capture history and go\r<br>backwards, in similar way as in survival - no problem with missing\r<br>visits there. But when we compute BOTH survival and recruitment, in\r<br>bayesian model we cannot go back and forth at the same time.\r<br>\r<br>So, now, having data with missing visits, and wanting to build an\r<br>integrated model - can this be done somehow in the time-symmetric\r<br>approach? Or do I have to just give up and duplicate the capture\r<br>histories, and compute survival on one copy of the data, and\r<br>recruitment on another (reading it from backwards) in the same\r<br>integrated model?\r<br>\r<br>Thanks!\r<br>\r<br>Tomas\r<br>\r<br>PS: here are the biased results of the 10.3.1 model with missing data,\r<br>as I described:\r<br>\r<